# What the image does not need, which is nearly everything.
#
# The build context is sent to the daemon in full before a single instruction
# runs, so this file is the difference between a build that uploads a venv and
# one that uploads the source. `.venv/` is the one that matters - it is hundreds
# of megabytes of a Linux environment that the image installs its own copy of
# from `requirements.txt` - and it is why this file exists at all.

# The development environment, rebuilt inside the image from requirements.txt.
.venv/

# History, and the identity of whoever built the image.
.git/
.gitignore

# The test suite. `requirements-dev.txt` is not installed either: a deployment
# does not carry a test runner, and pytest with nothing to collect is a lie
# about what the image can do.
tests/
.pytest_cache/
requirements-dev.txt
pyproject.toml

# Documentation and the checklist. Read by people, mounted by nothing.
docs/
README.md
claude.md

# **The environment file, which must never reach an image.** A secret baked into
# a layer is a secret in every registry that layer was pushed to, and `docker
# history` does not forget. The negation below is the same one `.gitignore`
# carries, for the same reason: the file with no values in it is documentation
# and belongs in both.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Databases, journals and backups. The image is expected to be pointed at a
# mounted `/data`, and a copy of a real database inside a built image would be
# exactly the mistake the bind mount exists to avoid.
*.db
*.db-journal
*.db-wal
*.db-shm
data/

# Bytecode, caches, and whatever an editor left behind.
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.vscode/
project.zip
